  "content": {
    "title": "Can you still file taxes late after April 15? Penalties, what IRS says to do",
    "text": "April 13, 2026, 11:58 a.m. ETThe federal tax deadline for 2025 returns is Wednesday, April 15, 2026.Taxpayers who miss the deadline without an extension may face penalties and interest.An extension to file until October 15 can be requested, but any taxes owed are still due by April 15.There is no penalty for filing late if you are owed a refund from the IRS.With the April 15 federal tax deadline nearly here, the IRS urges taxpayers to file as soon as possible, even if the deadline passes.The IRS has processed more than 78 million individual federal income tax returns so far in 2026. The agency says it has issued 57 million refunds via direct deposit, and that the average refund has increased by more than 10 percent, with total refunds topping $202 billion.Still, some taxpayers may need more time to file after April 15. Here's what taxpayers should know about filing after the deadline.When is the last day to file taxes in 2026?Taxpayers have until Wednesday, April 15, 2026, to file their 2025 tax returns and pay any tax owed, according to the IRS.What happens if you file your taxes late?Taxpayers who don’t file and pay by April 15 could face penalties and interest, according to the IRS.The IRS adds that taxpayers who cannot file their return by April 15 should request an extension by that date to avoid a failure to file penalty. An approved extension gives taxpayers until Oct. 15 to submit their return. However, any tax owed is still due April 15, and an extension to file is not an extension to pay.What are the penalties if you file your taxes late and don't pay?Taxpayers who miss the April 15 filing deadline should submit their tax return as soon as possible, the IRS notes. Filers may face penalties, which the IRS charges monthly until the full amount owed is paid.The standard penalty for filing late is five percent of any tax due for each month the return is late, up to 25% of the unpaid balance, according to USA TODAY.If you file a return but fail to pay any taxes you owe, or if you get an extension, you may face a smaller monthly penalty of 0.5% of any unpaid amount.When is the deadline to request an extension on taxes?You must file a tax extension by the regular tax filing deadline, which is April 15. This gives six more months (October 15) to file without penalties. However, an extension to file is not an extension to pay if you owe money to the IRS.Those who can’t pay the full balance by April 15 should still pay what they can and apply for a payment plan. Most applicants receive immediate notification of approval or denial.How do you request an extension with the IRS?The IRS offers three ways to request an extension:Pay online and select \"extension\": Make an online payment and choose “extension” as the reason. You’ll receive a confirmation number, and no additional forms are required.Use IRS Free File: Any taxpayer can use the program to request a free, automatic extension. Income limits apply only when using Free File to submit a tax return.File Form 4868: Submit Form 4868 by mail, through an IRS e-filing partner or with a tax professional. You must estimate your total tax owed and pay any remaining balance.Where is my tax refund? How to check status with IRSThe IRS says taxpayers who are owed a refund won't have to pay penalties for filing after the April 15 deadline, but should still file as soon as possible.Those who e-filed tax returns should receive a refund about one month or less from the filing date, according to the IRS. You can typically check your refund status soon after you file using the Where's My Refund tool, downloading the free IRS2Go mobile app or calling 800-829-1954.The refund tracker displays three status types:Return received: The IRS has received and is processing your tax return.Refund approved: If the return is complete and accurate, the IRS will approve and prepare to issue a refund.Refund sent: The refund was sent to your bank or the IRS sent a check through the mail.A refund status will appear 24 hours after you e-file a current-year return, three to four days after e-filing a prior-year return, or four weeks after you file a paper return.Typically, it takes up to 21 days to process an e-filed return, or longer for amended returns and returns sent by mail. The IRS recommends direct deposits as the fastest way to get your refund, rather than a mailed paper check that can take weeks to arrive.",
